    "['service-location [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'service-insertion service-location 1', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'service-insertion service-location 1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nservice-insertion service-location 1\\n##\\n\\nNote: Cisco's implementation of service location/chaining is different from Huawei's architecture. The closest equivalent would be Cisco's Service Insertion Architecture (SIA) or service-path commands, but exact feature parity doesn't exist. The above translation represents the conceptual mapping of defining a service location point.\", 'trans_template': 'service-insertion service-location 1'}]]": 1,
    "['locate slot [parameter1] engine [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'service location 1 1/0', 'parent_command': ['service-insertion service-location 1'], 'root': 'service-insertion service-location 1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nservice location 1 1/0\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\nThe Huawei command `locate slot 1 engine 0` specifies the service location on slot 1, engine 0. In Cisco terminology:\\n- \"slot\" translates to the slot/module number\\n- \"engine\" typically refers to the RP (Route Processor) or ESP (Embedded Services Processor)\\n\\nThe Cisco equivalent uses the format `service location <group-id> <slot>/<module>` where:\\n- `1` is the service location group ID (matching Huawei\\'s)\\n- `1/0` represents slot 1/module 0 (equivalent to Huawei\\'s slot 1/engine 0)', 'trans_template': 'service location 1 1/0'}]]": 1,
