    "['traffic-policy [parameter1] [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'service-policy input p1', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nservice-policy input p1\\n##  \\n\\nExplanation:  \\nIn Cisco IOS, the equivalent of Huawei's `traffic-policy p1 inbound` is `service-policy input p1` applied to an interface. Both commands attach a traffic policy (QoS policy in Cisco) to the inbound direction of an interface.  \\n\\nKey differences:  \\n- Huawei uses `traffic-policy` while Cisco uses `service-policy`  \\n- Huawei uses `inbound` while Cisco uses `input` (outbound would be `output` in Cisco)  \\n- The policy name (`p1`) remains the same in both contexts.  \\n\\nNote: This assumes the Cisco QoS policy `p1` has already been configured with equivalent class-maps and policy-maps matching the Huawei traffic classifier (`c1`) and behavior (`b1`) structure.\", 'trans_template': 'service-policy input p1'}]]": 1,
